## [upstream] specfunc: propagate non-finite arguments in gsl_sf_hypot_e

Commit: 1a470222a

`gsl_sf_hypot_e` computes `min/max` with `GSL_MIN_DBL`/`GSL_MAX_DBL`,
which compare with `<` and `>`.  A NaN compares false against
everything, so it is never selected and both aliases land on the same
argument.  Measured on the built library before the fix:

    gsl_sf_hypot(NaN, 1.0) -> val 1.4142135623730951, err 6.28e-16, SUCCESS
    gsl_sf_hypot(1.0, NaN) -> val inf, err inf, GSL_EOVRFLW
    gsl_sf_hypot(inf, 1.0) -> val inf, err inf, GSL_EOVRFLW

The first is the reported case: `min = max = 1.0` gives `sqrt(2)`.  The
other two reach the overflow test because `inf < DBL_MAX/root_term` is
false for an infinite `max`.  The C99 specification requires `hypot` to
return `+Inf` for an infinite argument and NaN for a NaN argument, and
never to raise a range error for a finite result.

Both cases are now handled at the top of the function: any infinite
argument returns `+Inf` with `err = 0`, otherwise a NaN argument
propagates as NaN, both with status `GSL_SUCCESS`.  No `isnan` guard is
added to the arithmetic itself - the two checks are the domain-specific
exception handling the standard defines for `hypot`, not a general NaN
trap, and they run only for the two non-finite arguments.

Tests: six vectors are added to `test_trig()` in `specfunc/test_sf.c`.
Negative control: with the guard reverted `specfunc_test` reports six
failures, all in the new vectors; with it applied the suite passes and
`ctest` is 56/56 on MSVC.

Documentation: `.. Exceptional Return Values: none` and the non-finite
rule are added to `gsl_sf_hypot`/`gsl_sf_hypot_e` in
`doc/specfunc-trig.rst` and `doc_texinfo/specfunc-trig.texi`.

Files: `specfunc/trig.c`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`, `doc/specfunc-trig.rst`,
`doc_texinfo/specfunc-trig.texi`.

