## [upstream] specfunc: reject the poles of 1F1 at x = 0

Commit: 1f2607a0b

`gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1_e()` and `gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1_int_e()` handled
`x == 0` before any parameter check and returned 1, so a pole was
reported as `1F1(a,b,0) = 1`:

    gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1(2, 0, 0)      -> 1
    gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1(2, 0, 1)      -> domain error
    gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1_int(2, 0, 0)  -> 1
    gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1(-3, -1, 0)    -> 1

The confluent hypergeometric function has a pole whenever `b` is zero
or a negative integer, except when the series terminates before the
denominator vanishes, i.e. when `a` is a nonpositive integer with
`a >= b`, in which case the value is finite.  The `x == 0` branch now
applies the classification the `x != 0` branches already used:
`GSL_EDOM` for `b == 0`, or for `b` a negative integer that `a` cannot
cancel (`a` not a nonpositive integer, or `a < b`).  The terminating
cases keep their value of 1, and `gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1(0, 0, 0)` becomes a
domain error to agree with `gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1(0, 0, x != 0)`.  The
integer form tests `a == b` first, so `gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1_int(0, 0, 0)`
remains 1, matching `gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1_int(0, 0, x != 0) = exp(x)`.

Negative control: with `specfunc/hyperg_1F1.c` reverted, the eight new
`TEST_SF_RETURN` vectors in `specfunc/test_hyperg.c` all fail with
"unexpected return code: 0"; with the fix the `specfunc` suite and the
full `ctest` are 56/56 on MSVC.

Documentation: both `specfunc-hyperg` entries in `doc/specfunc-hyperg.rst`
and `doc_texinfo/specfunc-hyperg.texi` now state the domain and the
terminating exception.

Files: `specfunc/hyperg_1F1.c`, `specfunc/test_hyperg.c`,
`doc/specfunc-hyperg.rst`, `doc_texinfo/specfunc-hyperg.texi`.

