## [upstream] cdf: make the gamma quantile solver robust

Commit: 9befdae95

`gsl_cdf_chisq_Pinv (0.5, 0.01)` aborted with "inverse failed to
converge".  The call reduces to `gsl_cdf_gamma_Pinv (0.5, 0.005, 2)`,
whose initial-approximation branching is only valid for shape `a >= 1`.
For `a = 0.005` it started at `x0 = a = 0.005`, and the damped
fixed-step iteration moved `x` by roughly halving on every step, so
after the 32 allowed iterations it was still at `2.8e-11` while the
true median is `3.51e-61`.

The expected value quoted in the report, `0.99477710813146`, is not the
inverse at all: it is `gsl_cdf_chisq_P (0.5, 0.01)`, the forward CDF at
`x = 0.5`.  The inverse is `7.016667765235591e-61` (mpmath, 60 digits).

`gsl_cdf_gamma_Qinv` has the same defect and, unlike `_Pinv`, no
convergence check, so it returned a silently wrong value: with
`a = 0.65`, `gsl_cdf_gamma_Qinv (5.8402405187288964e-219, 0.65, 1.0)`
gave `246.7` instead of `500` (chisq `493.4` against an expected
`1000`).  That vector is present in the generated `test_auto` tests and
now passes.

The fixed-step iteration is replaced by a bracketed Pegasus (regula
falsi) solver on `t = log(x)`.  The signed residual is monotone in `t`
with limits `-target` and `1 - target`, so a bracket always exists in
the representable range; the initial estimate uses the small-x and
Gaussian approximations and the bracket is closed by geometric
expansion.  The two entry points now delegate to whichever tail is no
larger than one half, so the inverted function is evaluated where it is
relatively accurate.  The forward incomplete gamma is called through
the status-returning routines; where the large-x expansion of `Q` would
raise a GSL error (it does so even from the status entry point), the
complementary continued fraction for `P` is used instead.

Verified against an independent sweep of `scipy.special.gammaincinv`
over shapes `1e-3 .. 1e6` and many tail probabilities.  After the fix
the only differences are roots below the normal range (unrepresentable,
now reported as `0`) and points where GSL's own forward `gamma_Q` is
inaccurate (for `a = 0.001`, `Q = 1e-12`, GSL's forward value is off by
about `3e-4`, so no inverse can do better).  Negative control: with
`cdf/gammainv.c` reverted, the new `cdf/test.c` vectors abort the `cdf`
suite at `gammainv.c:111`.  Full `ctest` is 56/56 on MSVC.

