## [upstream] specfunc: Cauchy principal value for the elliptic integrals of the third kind

Commit: 2673ce0d8

`gsl_sf_ellint_Pcomp_e` is what the report names, but the numbers in it
are the *incomplete* `gsl_sf_ellint_P(phi, k, n)`: the reporter's
`Pcomp(1.3, 0.5, -0.1)` equals `P_e(1.3, 0.5, -0.1) = 1.435170...`,
which is `EllipticPi(0.1, 1.3, 0.25)` in Wolfram's convention (GSL's
`n` is the negative of A&S's, as the manual states).

Both functions evaluate `Pi` through Carlson's `RJ`, whose `p` argument
is `1 + n sin^2(phi)` (respectively `1 + n`).  That argument is
negative exactly when the integrand has a pole in the interval of
integration (`n < -csc^2(phi)`), and `gsl_sf_ellint_RJ_e` rejected
`p < 0` with `GSL_EDOM`, so the result was `NaN`:

    P_e    (1.3, 0.5, -0.1)  ->  1.4351704933438834   (no pole)
    P_e    (1.3, 0.5, -1.1)  ->  NaN                  (pole, PV = 4.721120537361089)
    Pcomp_e(0.5,      -1.1)  ->  NaN                  (pole, PV = -0.240846396458214)

The ordinary Riemann integral diverges at such a pole, but the Cauchy
principal value is well defined and is what Wolfram Alpha and
Boost.Math return.

`gsl_sf_ellint_RJ_e()` now handles `p < 0` with the transformation
[Carlson, Numer. Math. 33 (1979) 1; DLMF 19.20.14], the same one
Boost.Math uses: sort `x <= y <= z`, put `q = -p` and
`p' = (z(x + y + q) - xy)/(z + q)`, then

    R_J(x, y, z, -q) = [(p' - z) R_J(x, y, z, p') - 3 R_F(x, y, z)
                        + 3 sqrt(xyz/(xy + p'q)) R_C(xy + p'q, p'q)]
                       / (z + q).

This fixes `P`, `Pcomp` and any direct `RJ` caller in one place.
`p = 0` stays `GSL_EDOM`, since the integral genuinely diverges there.

The change also fixes a pre-existing error estimate in
`gsl_sf_ellint_P_e()`: the two terms carrying the characteristic were
propagated with the signed `n/3`, so for `n < 0` the reported error
could be negative or much too small.  They now use `|n/3|`.

Verification: `mpmath.elliprj` at 40 digits is the independent
reference (its real part is the principal value); evaluated with the
same double-precision arguments it agrees with the library to 2.6e-15
over 25 random `p < 0` cases, and gives Wolfram's 4.72112 at the
reported point.  Five vectors are added to `test_ellint()` in
`specfunc/test_sf.c`.

Negative control: with the RJ branch disabled, four of the five new
vectors fail (`P_e(1.3,0.5,-1.1)`, `P_e(1.0,0.5,-3.0)`,
`Pcomp_e(0.5,-1.1)`, `RJ_e(0.2,0.5,1.0,-0.2)`); the fifth,
