## [upstream] specfunc: restore the x^(1-c) factor in the 2F1 renorm functions

Commit: 46b7c412e

`gsl_sf_hyperg_2F1_renorm_e()` and `gsl_sf_hyperg_2F1_conj_renorm_e()`
continue `2F1(a,b;c;x)/Gamma(c)` to negative integer `c` with the
[Abramowitz+Stegun, 15.1.2] relation.  That relation carries a factor
`x^(1-c)` which the code dropped, so the returned value was too large by
`x^(c-1)`:

    renorm(1,2,-3,0.4)   2572.016   instead of  65.8436
    renorm(5,5,-1,0.5)   4.95e6     four times too large
    renorm(5,5,-10,0.5)  1.39e20    2048 times too large

The same omission was present in the conjugate-parameter variant.  Since `c`
is a negative integer in this branch, `1-c` is a positive integer, so the
factor is evaluated with `gsl_sf_pow_int_e()` and its error is propagated
into the prefactor.  The early-termination branch (where a non-positive
integer `a` or `b` cancels the pole in `c`) returns zero and is unaffected,
because the polynomial value carries no `c`-pole to begin with.

This closes Savannah bug #53876.

The existing renorm vectors at `c = -1`, `-10` and `-100` had been generated
from the buggy code and therefore encoded the missing factor; they are
corrected here.  The corrected values come from the A&S limit evaluated
independently with mpmath at 50 digits - `hyp2f1` itself cannot be used
because `Gamma(c)` is a pole, so the prefactor
`Gamma(1+a-c)Gamma(1+b-c) x^(1-c) / (Gamma(a)Gamma(b)Gamma(2-c))` and the
remaining `2F1(1+a-c, 1+b-c; 2-c; x)` are formed separately.  The old and new
values differ by exactly `x^(c-1)` (a factor of 4 for `c = -1`, 2048 for
`c = -10`), which is the signature of the missing term.

Negative control: with the factor reverted, `specfunc_test` reports 8
failures - the seven renorm vectors and the summary line.  With it applied
the `specfunc` suite and the full CTest suite pass.

Files: `specfunc/hyperg_2F1.c`, `specfunc/test_hyperg.c`.

See Savannah bug #53876.


