## [upstream] specfunc: hyperg_U for x < 0 with non-integer a and integer b

Commit: 00859f816

`hyperg_U_negx()` reduces `U(a,b,x)` for `x < 0` with the
[Abramowitz+Stegun 13.1.3] connection formula.  Both of its terms carry a
denominator that vanishes when `b` is a positive integer: `Gamma(1-b)` in
the first and `Gamma(2-b)` in the second.  When `a` is not an integer the
two poles do not cancel, and the call fails outright:

    gsl_sf_hyperg_U(-0.5, 1, -1)   GSL_EDOM      poch(a,-a) = Gamma(0)/Gamma(a)
    gsl_sf_hyperg_U(-0.5, 2, -1)   GSL_EUNIMPL
    gsl_sf_hyperg_U(-0.5, 3, -1)   GSL_EUNIMPL

These are the cases Savannah bug #30510 reports (the `b = 1` hole and the
`b >= 2` integer limit).

The integer-`b` case `b = n+1`, `n >= 0`, is now evaluated with the DLMF
13.2.9 limit

    U(a,n+1,x) = (-1)^(n+1)/(n! Gamma(a-n))
                   sum_{k>=0} (a)_k/((n+1)_k k!) x^k
                     (ln x + psi(a+k) - psi(1+k) - psi(n+k+1))
               + 1/Gamma(a) sum_{k=1}^n (k-1)! (1-a+k)_{n-k}/(n-k)! x^(-k),

with `ln x` replaced by `ln|x|`.  For `x < 0` the principal value of `U`
is complex - it has a branch cut on the negative real axis - and `ln|x|`
selects its real part, the continuous real solution of Kummer's equation,
which is the value a real-valued routine has to return.  The new path is
confined to `|x| <= 100` and `n <= 170`; outside that range the previous
`GSL_EDOM` / `GSL_EUNIMPL` behaviour is kept rather than overflow the
series.

Correctness was established independently three ways.  `mpmath.hyperu`
at 50 digits agrees with the formula for `x > 0`; for `x < 0` the
`ln|x|` value equals the real part of the principal value, confirmed by
integrating Kummer's ODE from `x > 0` to `x < 0` through the upper
half-plane (the integration reproduces `mpmath.hyperu` to 12 digits).  A
660-point grid (`a` in `[-12.5, 3.5]`, `b` in `[1,12]`, `x` in
`[-0.5, -20]`) has a worst ratio of true error to reported error of 0.61,
so the error estimate remains honest even where the series cancels.

Negative control: with the new branch removed, `specfunc_test` reports 13
failures - the twelve new vectors and the summary line.  With it the
`specfunc` suite and the full CTest suite pass.

Files: `specfunc/hyperg_U.c`, `specfunc/test_hyperg.c`.

See Savannah bug #30510.


## [upstream] specfunc: propagate NaN in gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1_e

Commit: 99a73dd36

`gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1_e()` classified its parameters and fell through to the
generic evaluation without ever inspecting them for a NaN.  With
`b = NaN` the classification marks nothing, `b > 0.0` is false, and the
`b < 0.0` branch calls `hyperg_1F1_ab_neg()`, which with `x = -1` reaches
`hyperg_1F1_U()`.  That routine evaluates `M(ap,bp,x)` for
`ap = a-b+1 = NaN`, `bp = 2-b = NaN`, and calls `gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1_e()`
again with the same `b = NaN`.  The two functions recurse into each other
with no base case:

    gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1_e(1.0, NAN, -1.0, &r)
      -> hyperg_1F1_ab_neg -> hyperg_1F1_U -> gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1_e
      -> hyperg_1F1_ab_neg -> ...            (stack overflow)

Measured on the built DLL under ctypes: the call raises
`OSError: stack overflow` in the caller and, run as a process, dies with
`STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW` (`0xC00000FD`).  The reporter's `NAN` was a
compile-time constant in the `GSL.jl` test that found it.

The fix is the same shape as `#57978` and `#57979`: a NaN argument
propagates.  `gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1_e()` now returns `NaN`/`NaN` with
`GSL_SUCCESS` for a NaN `a`, `b` or `x`, before any classification.  This
is not a domain error - a NaN is not an argument outside the domain, it
is an unknown one, and everywhere else in `specfunc` it flows through the
arithmetic rather than being trapped.  The earlier `#58032` fix for the
`x = 0` poles is unaffected: it classifies with `b < 0` and `b == 0`,
both false for NaN, so the NaN check has to come first.

Negative control: with the guard disabled the three new vectors are not
merely reported wrong, the test program aborts with a stack overflow, so
they cannot pass by accident.  With it `specfunc_test` and the full CTest
suite are green.

Files: `specfunc/hyperg_1F1.c`, `specfunc/test_hyperg.c`, plus
`<gsl/gsl_sys.h>` for `gsl_isnan`.

See Savannah bug #55687.


## [upstream] specfunc: finite limits for Si and Ci at infinity

Commit: 3afec212b

`gsl_sf_Si_e()` and `gsl_sf_Ci_e()` take the `|x| > 4` asymptotics for an
infinite argument.  The asymptotics form `f cos(x) + g sin(x)` with
`f, g` finite, and `cos(inf)`/`sin(inf)` are NaN, so `Si(+inf)`,
`Si(-inf)` and `Ci(+inf)` all returned NaN.

Both functions have finite limits at infinity, so by the rule applied to
`#57978` and `#57979` these are non-finite arguments *with* a limit and
belong to the domain:

    Si(+inf) = +pi/2      Si is odd, so Si(-inf) = -pi/2
    Ci(+inf) = 0

The limit is now returned directly, with `GSL_SUCCESS`.  `Ci` has no
limit at `-inf`: the integral does not converge for `x < 0` and the
function is not defined there at all, so `Ci(-inf)` remains `GSL_EDOM`
via the existing `x <= 0` rule.  That asymmetry is deliberate and
mirrors the domain: Si is entire (odd, with limits at both ends), Ci is
defined only for `x > 0` with a limit at one end.  A NaN argument still
propagates through the series and asymptotics.

The `+inf` branch was verified against values at large finite `x`: the
library already returned 1.5707963267075846 for `Si(1e10)` and
-4.9e-11 for `Ci(1e10)`, so the limits are the continuation of the
existing values, not an independent formula.  `Si(-1e10)` gives
-1.5707963267075846, the negative of `Si(1e10)`, confirming the odd
branch.

Negative control: with the two `GSL_POSINF` / `gsl_isinf` branches
disabled the three new vectors fail with NaN.  `specfunc_test` and the
full CTest suite are green with them.

Files: `specfunc/sinint.c`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`.

See Savannah bug #58064.


## [upstream] specfunc: gsl_sf_gegenpoly_n at lambda = 0

Commit: 01d7e7609

The Gegenbauer polynomial `C_n^(lambda)(x)` is identically zero for
`lambda = 0` and `n >= 1`.  The standard normalisation is
`C_n^(1/2) = P_n`; scaling to finite coefficients at `lambda = 0` forces
every polynomial of positive degree to vanish.  scipy's `gegenbauer` and
mpmath both return 0 for `n = 1` through `5` at `lambda = 0`.

GSL returned the value of the *limit* `lambda -> 0` instead, which is
`2 T_n(x)/n`, and is not the value at `lambda = 0`:

    gegenpoly_1_e(0, x)  =  2x                C_1^(0)(x) = 0
    gegenpoly_2_e(0, x)  =  2x^2 - 1          C_2^(0)(x) = 0
    gegenpoly_3_e(0, x)  =  x(4x^2/3 - 2)     C_3^(0)(x) = 0

and `gsl_sf_gegenpoly_n_e(n, 0, x)` for `n >= 4` built its recurrence on
those two seeds, so it returned `2 T_n(x)/n` as well.  The comment in the
code even labelled the branch `2 T_n(x)/n`, which is the limit rather
than the value.

All four now return 0 with `err = 0` for `lambda = 0` and `n >= 1`, and
`gsl_sf_gegenpoly_array()` fills its `n >= 1` entries with 0.  `n = 0`
(the value 1) and every `lambda != 0` are untouched: the existing vectors
at `lambda = -0.2, 1, 5, 100` all still pass.

Three existing vectors encoded the old limit values at `lambda = 0`
(`gegenpoly_1_e(0,1) = 2`, `_2_e(0,1) = 1`, `_3_e(0,1) = -2/3`); they
are corrected to 0, which is what the definition requires.  New vectors
cover `n = 1, 2, 3, 4, 100` at `lambda = 0`, for `|x| <= 1` and for `x`
outside the orthogonality interval, and the array form.  Reverting the
change makes every one of them fail.

Files: `specfunc/gegenbauer.c`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`.

See Savannah bug #58065.


## [rejected] specfunc: the "dropped NaN" reports for the n = 0 special cases

Not applied.  The reports are not bugs.

Jackson Vanover filed five related items (Savannah `#58060` to `#58063`)
arguing that `gsl_sf_laguerre_n(0, a, x)`, `gsl_sf_hyperg_U_int(0, 1, x)`,
`gsl_sf_hyperg_1F1_int(0, 1, x)`, `gsl_sf_hermite(0, x)`,
`gsl_sf_gegenpoly_n(0, lambda, x)` and `gsl_sf_bessel_zero_Jnu(nu, s)`
should reject a NaN argument with an error instead of returning `1` (or
`0`), because a NaN does not satisfy the documented domain restriction on
the second parameter.  He later conceded in a comment on `#58062` that
"1F1(0;b;x)=1 for all x if b != 0, so even if x is entirely
unspecified/unknown the result is 1".

The fork's rule, settled in `#57978` and applied again here, is that a
NaN argument propagates and is not a domain error.  Where a function has
an actual removing value at `n = 0`, returning it is exactly "propagate
the unknown only as far as it matters": the result does not depend on the
unknown parameter, so a finite answer is correct and a NaN would be
invented, not propagated.  This is the same semantics the fork's own
`#58032` fix gave `1F1_e` at `x = 0`, where the value 1 is returned for
the terminating cases.  Forbidding the NaN would also be inconsistent
with `gsl_sf_laguerre_n_e(1, NaN, x)`, which correctly returns NaN
because the value *does* depend on `a`.

What the reports do expose is a documentation gap: the manuals did not
state the `n = 0` value or the NaN rule, and `#58060`'s original example
even used the wrong function name.  That is fixed in commit `fc4059b12`;
the code is deliberately left alone.  `#58061`'s `U_int(0,1,x) = 1` and
`#58062`'s `1F1_int(0,1,x) = 1` are the correct limits for every `x`,
including `x = NaN` in the sense above.

`#58031` (`gsl_sf_bessel_Kn_scaled(0, +inf)` -> 0) is in the same group.
`exp(x) K_0(x) -> 0` is the limit, the return value 0 with `GSL_SUCCESS`
is correct, and the reporter's "indeterminate form 0 x inf" describes the
asymptotic expansion, not the limit of the scaled function.  A vector
documenting the value is added; no code change.

`#58064`'s `gsl_sf_gamma_inc_P/Q(+inf, x)`, `gamma_inc_P/Q(a, +inf)`,
`gamma_inc(a, +inf)`, `erf(+inf)`, `erfc(+inf)` and `poch(-inf, x)` cases
are also correct - each returns the finite limit - so only Si/Ci needed a
code change, and the rest are documented.

Kept here so the reports are not re-investigated.

See Savannah bugs #58031, #58060, #58061, #58062, #58063 and #58064.


