## [upstream] headers: rename include guards to avoid reserved identifiers

Commit: e40dc7ceb

GSL include guards began with a double underscore (`__GSL_MATH_H__`).  An
identifier that starts with an underscore and an uppercase letter is
reserved to the implementation, and in C++ any identifier containing a
double underscore is reserved as well, so a program that includes a GSL
header was relying on names it is not allowed to define.

Every guard drops its leading double underscore (`__GSL_MATH_H__` becomes
`GSL_MATH_H__`), the change the tracker discussion settled on; the
trailing double underscore is deliberately kept.  The same leading
removal is applied to the private guards `__ERROR_CBLAS*` and
`__ROOTS_H__`, to `build.h` (`__GSL_BUILD_H__`, `__GSL_INLINE_H__`,
which must stay in step with `gsl_inline.h`), to `gsl_version.h.in`, the
`contrib/wigner.h` guard and the two `ringbuf.c` includers.  The header
example in both design manuals is updated too; the historical ChangeLog
mention is left alone.  `__BEGIN_DECLS`/`__END_DECLS` are untouched.

The replacement was applied only to the enumerated guard tokens, never
by stripping `__` blindly, so compiler and language macros such as
`__cplusplus`, `__GNUC__` and `__attribute__` are unaffected.

Verified on MSVC x64 with a clean CMake build and 56/56 ctest, and by
compiling each installed public header twice on its own: 251 headers
pass, and the 15 `spmatrix` headers are not standalone-includable
(a pre-existing condition, unrelated to the guards).  Negative control:
restoring the old `#define` name in `gsl_vector_double.h` makes the
double-include check report "conflicting types for 'gsl_vector'".

Files: 278 headers, `.in` files and two design manuals.
See Savannah bug #36197.


