## [upstream] multiroots: avoid division by zero when the initial point is a root

Commit: 8640b846e

Two solvers divided by a quantity that is zero at a root.

`gnewton_set()` stores `phi = enorm(f)` from the fdf callback, but
`gnewton_iterate()` asks the plain f callback for the next residual.  When
the initial point is a root of fdf but not of f, `phi0` is zero and the
relative step reduction computes `theta = phi1/phi0`, so the iterate
becomes `NaN` (Savannah #42219).  The reduction is now taken only when
`phi0 > 0.0`; the full step is then `d = 0`, which leaves the point in
place and lets the driver's residual test finish.

The hybrid methods (`hybrid`, `hybrids`, `hybridj`, `hybridsj`) take a
zero dogleg step at a root.  `compute_wv()` then divides by the zero step
norm to form the rank-1 Jacobian update, which poisons the QR factors with
`NaN`; the reported symptom is a floating point exception only when traps
are enabled, because the residual test stops the driver before the `NaN`
can reach the result (Savannah #42220).  The iteration now returns
`GSL_SUCCESS` as soon as the residual norm is zero, and `compute_wv()`
leaves its update vectors at zero when the step norm is zero.

Both were reproduced with the reporters' own `bug_gnewton.c` and
`bug_hybrid.c`: without the fix gnewton returns `-nan(ind)`, and both
programs die with `0xC0000090` under `GSL_IEEE_MODE=trap-common`.

The regression tests in `multiroots/test.c` run all four hybrid solvers
with the initial guess at the root, plus gnewton with mismatched f/fdf
callbacks.  The invalid-operation trap is enabled while they run, since
the hybrid failure is otherwise masked by the residual test.  With the
fix reverted the test process is killed by the trap; with it the module
suite is 77/77 and `ctest` is 56/56.

Files: `multiroots/gnewton.c`, `multiroots/hybrid.c`,
`multiroots/hybridj.c`, `multiroots/dogleg.c`, `multiroots/test.c`.

See Savannah bugs #42219 and #42220.


## [upstream] ode: keep the final step within the requested interval

Commit: dda917296

`gsl_odeiv2_evolve_apply()` clamps the last step to `h = t1 - t0` and
then lets the stepper evaluate its final stage at `t0 + h`.  In floating
point `t0 + (t1 - t0)` is not in general equal to `t1`; integrating
backwards from 1 to `1e-12` gives `9.999778782798785e-13`, so the
right-hand side was called just past the final time (Savannah #66849).
The clamp now also treats `h == t1 - t0` as the final step, and backs `h`
off with `nextafter` until `t0 + h` can no longer pass `t1`.

That alone was not enough.  `bsimp` splits the step into sub-steps and
builds their times by repeated `t += h`, which drifts past `t0 + h` as
well; for the final step of the reproducer the accumulated time could
dip below `t1` (for `N = 10`, to `9.999778782798785e-13`).
`bsimp_step_local()` now computes each sub-step time directly from `t0`
and `h_total`, so the last evaluation lands exactly on `t0 + h`.  The
legacy `ode-initval` copies carry the same code and are changed with it.

The regression test integrates backwards from 1 to `1e-12` with a
right-hand side that records the range of times it is evaluated at, and
asserts that it never goes below `t1` and that the final time equals `t1`
exactly.  It runs for every stepper.  With the fixes reverted the new
check fails for essentially every stepper; with them `ode-initval2` is
1184/1184, `ode-initval` is 3542/3542 and `ctest` is 56/56.

Files: `ode-initval2/evolve.c`, `ode-initval2/bsimp.c`,
`ode-initval2/test.c`, `ode-initval/evolve.c`, `ode-initval/bsimp.c`,
`ode-initval/test.c`.

See Savannah bug #66849.


