## [upstream] specfunc: form the eta_int leading term with gsl_ldexp

Commit: f774247e7

`gsl_sf_eta_int_e(n)` for `n <= -99` evaluates
`eta(n) = -(2^(1-n) - 1) zeta(n)` and built the leading factor `2^(1-n)`
with `gsl_sf_exp_e()`, which returns a non-zero error estimate.  The
factor is a power of two and is exactly representable, so
`gsl_ldexp(1.0, 1-n)` removes the rounding error - and with it the
dominant term of the reported error.  At `n = -101` the estimate drops
from 5.56e-14 to 2.42e-14 relative (a factor 2.3), and the value itself
becomes correctly rounded: 2.75e-16 against a 40-digit reference, down
from 5.6e-14.

Two details: the exponent test is formed in `double` so that
`n = INT_MIN` cannot overflow the `int` subtraction, and beyond the
representable range (`1-n >= 1024`) the exponential path is kept, so the
`GSL_EOVRFLW` status is unchanged.

Regression: `specfunc/test_sf.c` now asserts that the reported error at
`n = -101` is below `3e-14` relative.  With the exponential restored it
is 5.56e-14 and the check fails.

Files: `specfunc/zeta.c`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`.

See Savannah bug #68398.


## [upstream] specfunc: replace the floor(x+0.5) rounding idiom with rint

Commit: b681165e1

`floor(x + 0.5)`, used to round a double to the nearest integer, is wrong
for two classes of argument: the largest double below 0.5 (the addition
rounds up to 1, so the result is 1 instead of 0) and odd integers in
`[2^52, 2^53)` (adding 0.5 rounds up to the next even integer).  The
live uses are replaced by `rint()`, which rounds to nearest with ties to
even without an explicit addition:

* `bessel.c`, `bessel_{Inu,Jnu,Knu,Ynu}.c`, `gamma.c` - integer-order
  splits;
* `fermi_dirac.c`, `hyperg_{0F1,2F1,U}.c` - "is this parameter an
  integer" tests;
* `ellint.c` - the number of half periods in the angular reduction;
* `hermite.c` - the `RND()` scaling-exponent macro;
* `hyperg_1F1.c` - the `b-a`-is-an-integer test and the integer-order
  helpers used by `hyperg_1F1_ab_negint`.

The `hyperg_1F1.c` site had a second defect: it was missing its `fabs()`
and so accepted *any* `b-a` whose fractional part exceeded 0.5 as an
integer.  The reconstruction was checked against a 14950-point sweep
(`a` in `[-50, 0]`, `b` in `[0.5, 6]`, `x` in `[1, 110]`) and produced no
value change anywhere: the affected range takes the same `hyperg_1F1_U`
fallback either way, so the fix is a latent-correctness one.

`specfunc/coulomb.c` deliberately keeps `floor(lam_F + 0.5)`.  That is a
round-half-up, not round-to-nearest, chosen so that
`lam_min = lam_F - N` lands on the well-conditioned `-1/2` branch;
`rint(1/2) = 0` would leave `lam_min = +1/2`, which the code declares
impossible and which returns `GSL_EDIVERGE`.  It is left as-is with a
comment explaining the requirement.

Observed effect: one sub-ulp result correction and one error estimate.
`gsl_sf_bessel_Ynu_e(0.5, 1.0)` moves from 3.3e-16 to 7.5e-16 relative
error, so it is added as a vector with the closed-form reference
`-sqrt(2/(pi x)) cos(x)`; and
`gsl_sf_coulomb_wave_FG_e(1, 0.5, 0.5, 0)` pins the round-half-up
branch.  No other tested value moved outside its tolerance.

Files: the `specfunc` sources listed above, `specfunc/test_bessel.c`,
`specfunc/test_coulomb.c`.

See Savannah bug #47348.


## [rejected] Group R: 40116, 45099, 68663, 68704

Commits: none.

Four reports from the remaining open list were examined and deliberately
left unchanged.  Recorded here so they are not re-investigated.

### #40116 - "possible error in integration routines"

The report questions `integration/qpsrt2.c` (`large_interval()`,
`increase_nrmax()`) against the QUADPACK original `dqagse`, specifically
the comment "test whether the interval to be bisected next is the
smallest interval".  GSL is a faithful restructuring of the Fortran:

* `large_interval(i)` is QUADPACK's `if(|b(maxerr)-a(maxerr)| > small)
  go to 90`; because every bisection halves the interval, the exact test
  `level[i] < maximum_level` is equivalent to the length comparison and
  avoids QUADPACK's lagging `small`;
* the `do 50 k = id, jupbnd` scan is `increase_nrmax()`.  `increase_nrmax()`
  returning 1 corresponds to `go to 90` - i.e. it *does* cycle the loop -
  and returning 0 corresponds to falling through to the extrapolation.

The report itself says "I don't have a buggy case to report", and none
was found: the loop does not stall.  Substituted QUADPACK's parallel
structure by inspection of the source, not a patch.  Not a bug.

### #45099 - "wrong formula for BFGS update in gsl_multimin?"

The concern is that the approximate Hessian is never stored, and is
therefore treated as the identity.  It is not stored - it is implicit in
the search direction `p`.  Expanding the dense BFGS inverse-Hessian
update with `H0 = I`,

    H = (I - rho s y^T)(I - rho y s^T) + rho s s^T

gives `H g = g - A s - B y` with `B = s.g / s.y` and
`A = -(1 + |y|^2 / s.y) B + y.g / s.y`.  That is *verbatim* the update in
`multimin/vector_bfgs.c` (the `p' = g1 - A dx - B dg` block) and in
`multimin/vector_bfgs2.c`.  The formula is the standard one; the concern
is a misunderstanding.  Not a bug.

