## [upstream] cblas: reorder the gemm loops to reuse a row of C

Commit: 39cde03e2

`gsl_blas_dgemm()` reaches the reference kernels in
`cblas/source_gemm_r.h`, which accumulated the product with the `k`
index outermost.  That streams the whole `C` matrix once per term of
the inner product, so no row of `C` stays resident across the `k` loop
and the `F`/`G` access pattern is poor.  The `i` and `k` loops are
swapped in the two `NoTrans` branches and the `i` and `j` loops in the
two `Trans` branches, so a row of `C` is visited once and kept in cache
while `F` and `G` are traversed.

The reordering is numerically neutral.  For every `C(i,j)` the terms are
still accumulated in ascending `k` order; only the traversal order of
the independent `(i,j)` pairs changes, so the result is bit-for-bit
identical to the previous code.  The existing `cblas` gemm cases already
covered all four transpositions in both orders, and a new test,
`cblas/test_gemm_loops.c`, additionally compares `cblas_dgemm()` against
a straightforward triple-loop evaluation of the same mapping, which is
what pins the loop swap to the correct indexing.

This is performance-only work.  It was taken after the fork's scope was
extended to admit numerically neutral performance fixes; the earlier
verdict in `SAVANNAH_REVIEW.md` had set it aside as out of scope.

Files: `cblas/source_gemm_r.h`, `cblas/test_gemm_loops.c`,
`cblas/tests.c`, `cblas/tests.h`, `cblas/Makefile.am`,
`cmake/gsl_tests.cmake`.

See Savannah bug #54925.


## [upstream] permutation: pick the successor with a single backward scan

Commit: c382ed86e

`gsl_permutation_next()` and `gsl_permutation_prev()` searched the whole
descending (respectively ascending) suffix for the element to exchange,
keeping the smallest candidate and re-testing it with a comparison that
is implied by the loop that located `i`.  Both routines now scan the
suffix from the right and take the first element that exceeds
(respectively falls below) `p->data[i]`, which is step L3 of Knuth's
Algorithm L.  The suffix is monotone, so the same `k` is chosen and the
generated permutations are unchanged, but the scan usually stops after
one or two steps rather than walking the entire suffix on every call.

The existing vectors only cover 120 permutations of five elements.  A
new block in `permutation/test.c` walks all `8! = 40320` permutations in
both directions and compares each step against an independent reference
implementation of the lexicographic successor and predecessor.

`#21833`, filed ten years earlier against the same function, alleged
quadratic worst-case behaviour for the enumeration.  The reversal work
summed over all `n!` permutations is amortised `O(1)` per call, not
quadratic, so nothing further was needed and the exhaustive forward and
backward walk is the record of that.

Performance-only work, taken under the same extended scope as the cblas
loop reorder above.  The earlier "no defect" verdict in
`SAVANNAH_REVIEW.md` stands for the algorithm; this only removes the
constant factor.

Files: `permutation/permutation.c`, `permutation/test.c`.

See Savannah bugs #51104 and #21833.


## [upstream] roots: skip the redundant false-position evaluation

Commit: 0697ba772

`falsepos_iterate()` evaluated `f` at the linear interpolation point and
then, when that step failed to halve the bracket, evaluated it again at
the bisection point.  On an almost linear function the interpolation
collapses onto an endpoint once that endpoint is within rounding of the
root, so the first evaluation repeats a value the solver already holds
and every iteration costs two evaluations instead of one.

The routine now reuses `f_lower` / `f_upper` when `x_linear` equals
`x_left` / `x_right`, and evaluates `f` only when the interpolated point
is distinct.  No interface changes are involved: the value skipped is
exactly the one the endpoint already carries, so the sign test below
sees the same input as before.  The report also asked for the user's
tolerance to be passed into the iteration routine, which is the complete
fix but needs an interface change; it is deliberately not done.

On the report's 64-bit test function the number of evaluations falls
from 98 to 52.  A regression test in `roots/test.c` wraps the same
almost linear function in an evaluation counter and requires at most 60;
with the skip removed the count is 98 and the test fails.

Files: `roots/falsepos.c`, `roots/test.c`.

See Savannah bug #40092.


