## [upstream] specfunc: evaluate 2F1 at the contiguous arguments c = a-1 and c = b-1

Commit: adc9389a4

For `c = a-1` or `c = b-1` the hypergeometric exponent difference
`d = c-a-b+1` vanishes, which is exactly the case `hyperg_2F1_reflect`
cannot handle.  When `c >= 0` the series path happened to return the
right value, but when `c < 0` the dispatch reached the general branch
and returned `val = 0, err = 0` — a silent wrong answer — or
`GSL_EUNIMPL`.  For example `2F1(0.7884, 20.77, -0.2116, 0.1203)` was
`0` where the value is `-177.83`.

The posted patch for this bug (Savannah attachment `specfunc_hyperg_2F1.diff`)
proposes `(1 - x(1 - b/a))/(1-x)^(a+1)`, which is **not** the value of
`2F1(a,b,a-1,x)`; applied as written it would have replaced correct
results by wrong ones at every `c = a-1`.  It was used only as a
diagnosis.  Solving DLMF 15.5.17

  (a-1+(b+1-c)z) F(a,b;c;z) + (c-a) F(a-1,b;c;z)
    - (c-1)(1-z) F(a,b;c-1;z) = 0

for `F(a,b;c-1)`, setting `c = a` and using `F(a,b;a;z) = (1-z)^-b`,
gives the closed forms

  2F1(a, b, a-1, x) = (a-1 + (b+1-a) x) / ((a-1) (1-x)^(b+1))
  2F1(a, b, b-1, x) = (b-1 + (a+1-b) x) / ((b-1) (1-x)^(a+1)),

valid for all `|x| < 1`.  They are evaluated with the existing
`pow_omx` helper so the error estimate is meaningful.  The `a = 0` and
`b = 0` cases still terminate earlier; the `x < -1` / `x >= 1`
convergence check still applies.

Verification: both closed forms were checked against `mpmath.hyp2f1`
at 50 digits over a randomized parameter sweep, and against the DLMF
relation itself.  The expected values in the test are mpmath values at
30 digits and are independent of GSL.  Reverting the change reproduces
three failures: the two `c < 0` cases above and `2F1(3,7,2,-1/2)`.

Files: `specfunc/hyperg_2F1.c`, `specfunc/test_hyperg.c`.  `doc:`
mirrors the two identities in both manuals.

See Savannah bug #66850.


## [upstream] specfunc: take the exact value of 2F0 when a or b is zero

Commit: 1d99544d9

`2F0(a,b,0) = 2F0(0,b,x) = 2F0(a,0,x) = 1`.  The `x == 0` test used to
sit *after* the `x < 0` route, which evaluates
`U(a,1+a-b,-1/x)`; that asymptotic form loses the vanishing parameter
by cancellation, so e.g. `2F0(200,0,-0.001)` returned `NaN` although the
value is exactly `1`.  Test `a == 0 || b == 0` before the `x < 0`
branch.  This is the clean part of the posted patch.

The `a` or `b` zero case with `x >= 0` is still `GSL_EDOM`: the defining
series only converges at `x = 0`, and returning `1` for `x > 0` would be
analytic continuation, not a bug fix, so it is deliberately not taken.

Verification: the built library returns `NaN` for `2F0(200,0,-0.001)`
before the change and `1` after.  Two of the new vectors fail with the
change reverted.

Files: `specfunc/hyperg_2F0.c`, `specfunc/test_hyperg.c`.  Documentation
updated in both manuals.

See Savannah bug #67359.


## [upstream] specfunc: return -Inf for complex_logsin at a real zero of sin

Commit: 10d64fb15

At `zi = 0` with `zr` an exact multiple of pi, `sin(zr)` is zero and
`log(sin z)` diverges to `-Infinity` along the real axis.
`gsl_sf_complex_logsin_e` reached `gsl_sf_complex_log_e(0,0)` and
returned `NaN` with `GSL_EDOM`; `gsl_sf_complex_logsin(0,0)` was NaN.

The posted patch tested `fmod(zr, M_PI)` against `GSL_DBL_EPSILON`.
Because `M_PI` is only an approximation to pi this both misses some
exact multiples in floating point and fires on nearby non-multiples, and
it forces `lszi = 0` where the branch of the imaginary part is a genuine
discontinuity.  Instead the limit is taken only when the library's own
`gsl_sf_sin(zr)` returns exactly `0.0`, which is precisely where
`sin(zr)` vanishes in double precision; `zr = 0` is the common case and
the branch value `-Inf, 0` is returned there with zero error.  Values
off the exact zeros, including the `zr = pi` that a naive `fmod` would
mangle, are unchanged.

Verification: the built library returns `(NaN, NaN)` for `(0,0)` before
the change and `(-Inf, 0)` after; the new `(0,0)` vector fails with the
change reverted.

Files: `specfunc/trig.c`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`.  Documentation updated
in both manuals.

See Savannah bug #66922.


## [upstream] randist: handle p = 1 and p = 0 in negative_binomial_pdf

Commit: 957708351

The negative binomial with `p = 1` is a point mass at `k = 0`: the only
outcome is "no failures before the first success".  The general formula
`p^n (1-p)^k` computed `n*log(1) = 0` and `k*log1p(-1) = -Inf`, giving
`NaN` at `k = 0` (`k > 0` was already `0`).  `p = 0` gave `NaN` for
every `k`.  Return `1` at `k = 0` and `0` otherwise for both, which is
the limit of the mass function.  This is the intent of the posted
patches, which were syntactically broken (they referred to an
undeclared `i`).

No out-of-domain guard for `p` outside `[0,1]` is added; a NaN input is
left to propagate, as elsewhere.

Verification: the built library returns `NaN` for `(k=0, p=1, n=5)`
before the change and `1` after.  The direct test fails with the change
reverted.

Files: `randist/nbinomial.c`, `randist/test.c`.  Documentation updated
in both manuals.

See Savannah bug #66800.


## [upstream] specfunc: require a usable error bar for infinite results in test_sf

Commit: d00104093

`test_sf_check_result()` short-circuited into the `isnan`/`isinf`
comparison and never looked at `r.err` when both the expected and the
obtained value were non-finite.  A result of `+Inf` with `err = NaN`
therefore passed: `gsl_sf_erf_Z_e(+Inf)` is exactly that shape.  The
`isinf` branch now checks `r.err` for NaN and for a negative value, in
the same way as the finite branch.  An infinite `err` is left alone, as
it is the legitimate signal for an overflow.  No existing expectation
changes.

Files: `specfunc/test_sf.c`.

See Savannah bug #66842.


## [upstream] specfunc: continue 2F1 to x < -1 by the Pfaff transformations

Commits: b99ec4e7c (real), cfe9311cd (conjugate)

`gsl_sf_hyperg_2F1_e()` and `gsl_sf_hyperg_2F1_conj_e()` evaluate the
Gauss series, which converges only for `|x| < 1`, and rejected `x < -1`
with `GSL_EDOM`.  The report asks for the range to be extended with the
two Pfaff transformations (MathWorld relations 32/33, DLMF 15.8.1 and
15.8.2), which map `x < -1` to an argument `z = x/(x-1)` in `(1/2, 1)`:

  2F1(a,b;c;x) = (1-x)^-a 2F1(a,   c-b; c, x/(x-1))
               = (1-x)^-b 2F1(c-a, b;   c, x/(x-1))

The branch is taken after the terminating-series dispatch for a
non-positive integer `a` or `b` (so those polynomials keep their exact
values) and is skipped at `x = -1`, leaving the previous behaviour
there untouched; `x >= 1` still raises `GSL_EDOM`.  For the real
function the form is chosen so that the transformed series terminates
when `c-b` or `c-a` is a non-positive integer and otherwise so that the
prefactor exponent is smallest; the prefactor is combined with
`gsl_sf_exp_mult_err_e`.

For the conjugate function the transformed pair `(aR+i aI, (c-aR)+i aI)`
is **not** a conjugate pair - both parameters carry the same imaginary
part - so `hyperg_2F1_conj_series` cannot be reused.  A new static
helper sums the Gauss series for that pair directly in complex
arithmetic and returns the real part of the product with the complex
prefactor (the imaginary part vanishes identically).  The `aI = 0` case
reduces to the real function and is delegated to it.
`gsl_sf_hyperg_2F1_renorm_e` inherits the real extension through its
generic-`c` path.

References were computed independently with `mpmath.hyp2f1` at 60
digits.  For the real function a 14-vector set (including the two
terminating cases, which are exact) agrees to a few ulp; for the
conjugate function a 750-point grid over `(aR, aI, c, x < -1)` was
checked and every call succeeds with the true error inside the reported
bar.  Negative controls: with the real branch removed `specfunc_test`
reports 9 vector failures plus the summary line; with the conjugate
branch disabled it reports 7 vector failures plus the summary line.
With both, the `specfunc` suite and the full CTest suite pass.

Files: `specfunc/hyperg_2F1.c`, `specfunc/test_hyperg.c`.  Both manuals
updated (`doc/specfunc-hyperg.rst`, `doc_texinfo/specfunc-hyperg.texi`).

See Savannah bug #30324.


## [upstream] randist: reject |rho| >= 1 in the bivariate Gaussian

Commit: 6da0c08f3

`gsl_ran_bivariate_gaussian_pdf()` divides by `sqrt(1 - rho^2)`, so at
the perfectly correlated limit `rho = +/-1` it returned `NaN`/`Inf`, and
for `|rho| > 1` it returned `NaN` through the square root of a negative
number.  There is no two-dimensional density at `|rho| = 1`, so the
documented range `-1 < rho < 1` is now enforced: the pdf signals
`GSL_EDOM` through the error handler and `gsl_ran_bivariate_gaussian()`
does the same before consuming any random numbers.  (The generator would
produce a valid degenerate pair at `rho = +/-1`, but it is guarded too so
the documented domain is consistent across the pair.)  The manuals said
only "rho should lie between 1 and -1", which does not say whether the
endpoints are included; both entries now give the open interval and the
pdf entry notes the domain error.

Negative control: with the two guards disabled the pdf returns `NaN`;
with them, the new `randist/test.c` check (a counting error handler,
`rho` in `{+/-1, +/-2}` must raise `GSL_EDOM` once and in-range values
must stay silent) fails, and the `randist` suite passes.

Files: `randist/bigauss.c`, `randist/test.c`.  Both manuals updated.

See Savannah bug #68098.


## [upstream] ode-initval: iterate the legacy rk2imp/rk4imp to convergence

Commit: fa1622e11

The legacy implicit Gauss steppers solved their stage equations with
three unconditional fixed-point iterations and carried the comment
"This method does not check for convergence".  That makes a symplectic
method behave like an explicit one and gives the energy error a secular
drift.  Reproduced on the Henon-Heiles Hamiltonian at `h = 0.1`: the
maximum `|H - H0|` grows from `1.4e-9` to `7.5e-9` (rk4imp) and from
`1.8e-5` to `4.3e-5` (rk2imp) between the first and the last 1000-time
window over `[0, 10000]`.

Both steppers now iterate until the stage increment satisfies
`|delta| <= GSL_DBL_EPSILON (|Y| + 1)`, with a cap of 1000 iterations and
a guard against non-finite iterates.  A step that does not converge
leaves `y` untouched and reports `yerr = GSL_DBL_MAX`, so the adaptive
control rejects it and reduces `h`.  Reporting a hard failure instead
would abort `gsl_odeiv_evolve_apply()` (unlike v2 it does not retry) and
would also break `gear2`, which uses `rk4imp` as its primer; the
non-converged iterate must not reach the control either, because a NaN
in `y` or `dydt_out` makes the control's error norm increase the step.

With the fix the error is bounded: rk4imp holds at about `1.0e-9` and
rk2imp at about `1.6e-5` across all ten windows.  The new
`test_energy_conservation()` asserts that the last window is no more than
twice the first.  Negative control: with the two files reverted the test
reports two failures (rk4imp `7.5e-9` vs `1.4e-9`, rk2imp `4.3e-5` vs
`1.8e-5`); with the fix the `ode-initval` and full CTest suites pass.

Files: `ode-initval/rk2imp.c`, `ode-initval/rk4imp.c`,
`ode-initval/test.c`.  The legacy v1 steppers have no per-stepper manual
entry (only the v2 replacements are documented), so no manual change.

See Savannah bug #30540.


## [upstream] specfunc: positive error bar for the arctangent integral at negative arguments

Commit: 4220f05d9

`gsl_sf_atanint_e()` evaluates its `|x| <= 1` branch as `val = x * c`
and `err = x * e`, where `c` and `e` come from a Chebyshev series in
`x*x`.  The value is therefore right for `x < 0` (the function is odd:
the series describes `AtanInt(|x|)` and the sign of `x` is carried by
the product), but the error estimate is not: multiplying the
non-negative series error by a signed `x` made it negative for every
`0.5*sqrt(eps) < |x| <= 1`.  Measured on the built library, `-1` gave
`-2.93e-17` and `-0.1` gave `-1.39e-18`; a negative error bar is
meaningless and also made the result fail the specfunc harness's
consistency check.

Use `ax` in the error expression.  The `|x| > 1` branch already reported
a non-negative error, so nothing else changes.  The argument range was
never restricted: the manual defines `AtanInt(x) = int_0^x
arctan(t)/t` with an empty `Domain`, and the code already handled
negative `x`; this is a defect in the existing behaviour, not new API.

Verification: the new vectors `(-0.1, -1.0, -2.0, 0.0)` pass the
specfunc harness.  With the one-line change reverted the `(-0.1)` and
`(-1.0)` vectors fail on both the negative error and the consistency
check, while `(-2.0)` is unaffected because it takes the `|x| > 1`
branch.  The expected values were checked independently against mpmath
`quad(atan(t)/t, [0, x])` at 40 digits.  Full ctest 56/56.

Files: `specfunc/atanint.c`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`.  The manuals already
describe the integral over the whole line, so no prose change.

See Savannah bug #67774.


## [upstream] linalg: apply the Householder left transform in storage order

Commit: cd9e31326

`gsl_linalg_householder_hm()` applied `H = I - tau v v^T` one column at
a time.  GSL stores matrices row-major, so for a fixed column the inner
loop over rows stepped through memory with the row stride, and the whole
`O(m n^2)` work of the QR factorization was done in the least
cache-friendly order.  In the CMake/MSVC build it was worse still:
`HAVE_INLINE` is undefined there, so `gsl_matrix_get()/set()` and
`gsl_vector_get()` were genuine out-of-line calls in the innermost loop.

This is the dominant cost of `gsl_linalg_QRPT_decomp()`, which lmsder
re-enters on every successful iteration (`multifit/lmset.c`,
`multifit/lmiterate.c`), and it matches the 2007 report that the solver
spends most of its time in the Householder transform.  Nothing here
changes the algorithm; the work is simply carried out in storage order.

The non-BLAS branch now walks a block of 64 columns at a time using
direct row pointers.  The arithmetic applied to a single column is
unchanged - the dot product still accumulates in increasing row order
and each element is updated with the same expression - so the result is
bit-for-bit identical.  (The `USE_BLAS` branch is left alone; it is
never compiled by the fork's builds.)

Verification: on a 550 x 25 matrix the decomposition drops from `1.43`
ms to `0.21` ms per call (MSVC x64 Release, best of 5 x 400).  The full
matrix, `tau`, the permutation and the column-norm vector are
bit-identical before and after over six shapes (`5x4` up to `550x25`)
and three seeds.  Full ctest 56/56.  A bit-identical kernel has no
negative-control vector to add.

Files: `linalg/householder.c`.

See Savannah bug #21828.


## [upstream] cdf: Erlang cumulative distribution

Commit: 3d607fce8

The `randist` module has provided `gsl_ran_erlang()` for a long time but
the `cdf` module had no matching cumulative distribution.  Add
`gsl_cdf_erlang_P()` and `gsl_cdf_erlang_Q()` with shape `k` and rate
`lambda`; the Erlang distribution is the gamma distribution with shape
`a = k` and scale `b = 1/lambda`, so both delegate to
`gsl_cdf_gamma_P/Q()`.  `k < 1` and `lambda <= 0` raise `GSL_EDOM`.

Note that `gsl_ran_erlang(r, a, n)` takes the scale and the shape, in
that order, which is the reverse of the new `(k, lambda)` convention;
the manual points this out.

Verification: the new vectors are the exact Poisson sum
`P(x) = 1 - sum_{i=0}^{k-1} exp(-lambda x) (lambda x)^i / i!`, computed
independently with scipy, at `k = 1, 3, 10` and several `x`; reversing
the delegation makes the `Q` vectors fail.  Full ctest 56/56.

Files: `cdf/erlang.c`, `cdf/gsl_cdf.h`, `cdf/Makefile.am`, `cdf/test.c`,
`cmake/gsl_sources.cmake`, both manuals.

See Savannah bug #66949.


## [upstream] randist: Nakagami distribution

Commit: 999d53bbe

Add `gsl_ran_nakagami()` and `gsl_ran_nakagami_pdf()` for the Nakagami
distribution with shape `mu` and scale `omega`.  If `Y` is gamma
distributed with shape `mu` and scale `omega/mu` then `X = sqrt(Y)` is
Nakagami, so the generator is a square root of `gsl_ran_gamma()`; the
pdf is the defining formula.  The `x = 0, mu = 1/2` case is handled
separately because the exponent contains `0 * log(0)`.

Verification: pdf vectors at seven points were computed independently
with scipy/mpmath, the sampling is checked against the pdf and against
the analytic tail probability `P(0 < X < b) = gammainc(mu, mu b^2/omega)`,
and the tests run after every other distribution test so that the shared
RNG stream is not shifted (an earlier placement perturbed the
multivariate Gaussian test).  Multiplying the pdf by 1/2 instead of the
correct factor fails the reference vectors.  Full ctest 56/56.

Files: `randist/nakagami.c`, `randist/gsl_randist.h`,
`randist/Makefile.am`, `randist/test.c`, `cmake/gsl_sources.cmake`,
both manuals.

See Savannah bug #66816.


## [upstream] randist, cdf: truncated Gaussian distribution

Commit: 76f3b1db8

Add `gsl_ran_tgaussian()`, `gsl_ran_tgaussian_pdf()` and
`gsl_cdf_tgaussian_P/Q/Pinv/Qinv()` for the Gaussian distribution with
standard deviation `sigma` truncated to `[a, b]`.  The posted Savannah
patch put the generator in `randist` but had it call `gsl_cdf`, which
would create a `randist` -> `cdf` cycle (`cdf` already calls `randist`
from `betainv.c`, `hypergeometric.c` and `tdistinv.c`); it also fixed
only three of the affected autotools link lines.

The generator here uses rejection from the untruncated Gaussian and the
pdf normalizes with `Phi(b/sigma) - Phi(a/sigma)` computed from
`gsl_sf_erfc()`, so `randist` gains no dependency on `cdf`.  The cdf
functions reuse `gsl_cdf_gaussian_P/Q/Pinv` and are therefore free of
the cycle.  `a >= b` raises `GSL_EDOM`.

Verification: the P/Q/Pinv/Qinv vectors are the Burkardt table (pages
23-24), checked independently with mpmath; the sampling is checked
against the pdf and the exact tail probabilities, and the pdf against
six independently computed values.  The P/Q vectors use TEST_TOL2
because the difference of two Gaussians loses a few ulp.  Changing the
pdf normalization factor fails the reference vectors.  Full ctest
56/56.

Files: `randist/gauss.c`, `randist/gsl_randist.h`, `cdf/gauss.c`,
`cdf/gaussinv.c`, `cdf/gsl_cdf.h`, `cdf/test.c`, `randist/test.c`,
both manuals.

See Savannah bug #59900.


## [upstream] cdf: inverse binomial and Poisson distributions

Commit: e88a81c75

Add the discrete quantiles `gsl_cdf_binomial_Pinv/Qinv()` and
`gsl_cdf_poisson_Pinv/Qinv()`: the smallest integer `k` with
`P(k) >= P` (or `Q(k) <= Q`), returned as a `double`.  The binomial
search bisects the bounded range `0..n`; the Poisson support is
unbounded, so the upper limit is doubled until the tail condition holds
and then bisected.  For the Poisson distribution `P = 1` returns
`+Inf` and `P = 0` returns `0`; because the cdf takes an `unsigned int`
argument, the quantile saturates at `UINT_MAX`.

The posted attachments used a linear scan; a bisection is
`O(log n)` and gives the same integer.  The report's Poisson prototype
was a copy of the binomial one and is corrected to `(P, mu)`.

Verification: vectors are R's `qbinom`/`qpois` (the same quantile
convention) at several `(n, p)` and `mu`, plus exact small-case sums and
the edge values; shifting the bisection result by one fails the tests.
Full ctest 56/56.

Files: `cdf/binomialinv.c`, `cdf/poissoninv.c`, `cdf/gsl_cdf.h`,
`cdf/Makefile.am`, `cdf/test.c`, `cmake/gsl_sources.cmake`, both
manuals.

See Savannah bugs #66767 and #66775.


## [rejected] randist: gamma-tail distribution (Savannah #24252)

The 2008 attachment `gamma_tail_jpl_080908.c` (recovered from the
Wayback Machine) is a left-truncated gamma sampler
`devroye_tail_gamma_ran(rng, tail, a)` with unit scale.  It was proposed
again in a 2026 follow-up as a faster replacement for the `a < 1` branch
of `gsl_ran_gamma`.

It is not correct.  For `a >= 1` the branches reproduce the truncated
gamma, but the `a < 1` branch accepts with probability `(a/X)^(1-a)`
where the proposal `Y = tail + Exp(1)` requires `(tail/Y)^(1-a)`: the
constant must be `tail`, not `a`.  The two agree only when `tail = a`.
At `tail = 0` the branch is meaningless, and the claimed speed-up cannot
hold.  Simulated on 200000 draws, `a = 0.5, tail = 0` gives mean `0.75`
instead of `0.50`; the correct `gsl_ran_gamma` already uses the standard
`Gamma(1+a) * U^(1/a)` identity.

No change is made.  A correct truncated-gamma generator would be a
separate feature, and the posted code is not a usable basis for it.

See Savannah bug #24252.


## [upstream] specfunc: complex exponential integrals E_n(z)

Commits: c01e6b922, 4416fe729, ffad9c422

The library had real exponential integrals only.  Savannah bug #24871
(2008) asks for a complex argument and points at D. E. Amos,
"Computation of Exponential Integrals of a Complex Argument", ACM
Trans. Math. Software 16(2), 169-177, and Algorithm 683, ibid. 178-182
(1990).  The new `specfunc/expint_complex.c` adds

    gsl_sf_complex_expint_{E1,En,E1_scaled,En_scaled,Ei}_e

where `En(z)` is the analytic continuation of the real integral to the
cut plane `-pi < arg z <= pi` (negative real axis approached from
above), `_scaled` is `exp(z) En(z)`, and `Ei` uses
`Ei(z) = -E1(-z) + (log z - log(-z))`.

The implementation is a C translation of the double-precision
subroutines of the algorithm: a power series for `|z| <= 2`, a backward
(Miller) recurrence for the confluent hypergeometric representation
outside a strip about the negative real axis, and Taylor continuation
inside it.  The original sequence interface is reduced to a single
order, which is all the public API exposes; with a sequence length of
one the continuation only ever steps upward from `min(n, round(|z|))`,
so the downward-continuation branch is not needed.  The `I1MACH` /
`D1MACH` constants are expressed with `float.h`, and `DPSIXN` is
replaced by the `gsl_sf_psi_int` already in the library.  The Fortran
error flags map to `GSL_EDOM`, `GSL_EUNDRFLW`, `GSL_EOVRFLW`,
`GSL_ELOSS`, `GSL_EMAXITER` and `GSL_EFAILED`; a NaN argument
propagates, `z -> +inf` on the real axis gives zero, and any other
non-finite argument is a domain error.

Verification: the routine agrees with independent 40-digit `mpmath`
values to a maximum relative error of 1.1e-14 over a random sweep of
8000 points spanning the series region, both half planes, the Taylor
strip, both sides of the cut, large `|z|` and orders 1..60, and to the
same accuracy on the hardcoded grid.  On the negative real axis the
real part matches the real `E_n(x)` and the imaginary part the jump
`-pi (-x)^(n-1)/(n-1)!`; `Ei` matches `gsl_sf_expint_Ei` on the
positive axis.  The recurrence `n En+1(z) + z En(z) = exp(-z)` and the
scaling identity hold.  Negative control: mutating the sign in the
contiguous relation fails 31 of the new checks (30 vectors plus the
aggregate), while the unmutated tree is clean.  Full ctest 56/56 on
MSVC x64.

Files: `specfunc/expint_complex.c`, `specfunc/gsl_sf_expint.h`,
`specfunc/Makefile.am`, `cmake/gsl_sources.cmake`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`,
`specfunc/test_sf.h`, both manuals.

See Savannah bug #24871.


