## [upstream] specfunc: inverse Jacobi elliptic functions

Commit: 183ca894f (documentation: 653c77bca)

The library has the direct Jacobi elliptic functions
`gsl_sf_elljac_e(u, m, ...)` but no inverse.  Savannah bug #68367 (2026)
posts a prototype that inverts them through `gsl_sf_ellint_F_e()`.  Three
per-function routines with result/error bars were added instead:

    int gsl_sf_elljac_arcsn_e(double sn, double m, gsl_sf_result * result);
    int gsl_sf_elljac_arccn_e(double cn, double m, gsl_sf_result * result);
    int gsl_sf_elljac_arcdn_e(double dn, double m, gsl_sf_result * result);

with `double` wrappers.  Each returns the principal value `u` in
`[0, 2K(m)]` for the parameter `m` (`|m| <= 1`), so that
`gsl_sf_elljac_e(u, m, ...)` reproduces the argument.

The posted prototype is wrong: it passes `m` to `gsl_sf_ellint_F_e()`,
whose second argument is the modulus `k = sqrt(m)`, not the parameter.
It also returns success if any one output succeeds and hides failures.
The fork instead evaluates the reduced incomplete integral directly as
`F(phi|m) = sin(phi) RF(cos^2 phi, 1 - m sin^2 phi, 1)`, which uses the
parameter and therefore also covers `m < 0` (where the modulus is
imaginary and `gsl_sf_ellint_F_e()` cannot be used).  `K(m)` is
`RF(0, 1 - m, 1)`, real for every `m <= 1`.  `arcsn` uses
`phi = asin(sn)`; `arccn` uses `phi = acos(cn)` and, for `cn < 0`, the
reduction `u = 2K - F(pi - phi|m)`; `arcdn` uses
`sin^2(phi) = (1 - dn^2)/m`.  The inverse of `dn` is a domain error at
`m = 0`, where `dn(u|0) = 1` for every `u`.

Verification: reference values from 30-digit `mpmath` (`ellipf`) for
`m` in `{0, 0.3, 0.7, 0.99, -0.4, 1}` and both signs of the argument,
all within `TEST_TOL0`; a round trip through `gsl_sf_elljac_e` over the
same grid; and explicit `GSL_EDOM` cases.  Negative control: flipping
the sign of the `m` term inside `RF` fails the new checks, while the
restored tree is clean.  Full ctest 56/56 on MSVC x64.

