## [upstream] multiroots: configurable finite-difference Jacobian step

Commit: 8abbe2c30 (documentation: 296f3b58a)

The derivative-free multiroot solvers compute their Jacobian by finite
differences with a hard-coded relative step of
`GSL_SQRT_DBL_EPSILON` (`fdjac.c`).  Savannah bug #45782 (2015) points
out that when the function is only known to a limited accuracy the
default step can be smaller than the evaluation error, so the
difference quotient is dominated by noise.

The step is now stored in a new `double fdjac_epsrel` field of
`gsl_multiroot_fsolver`, initialised to `GSL_SQRT_DBL_EPSILON` in
`gsl_multiroot_fsolver_alloc()` and accessed through

    int gsl_multiroot_fsolver_set_fdjac_epsrel(gsl_multiroot_fsolver * s,
                                               double epsrel);
    double gsl_multiroot_fsolver_fdjac_epsrel(const gsl_multiroot_fsolver * s);

The value is threaded through the `gsl_multiroot_fsolver_type` `set`
and `iterate` callbacks to `gsl_multiroot_fdjacobian()` in the
`dnewton`, `broyden`, `hybrid` and `hybrids` solvers.  The callback
signatures gain a `double` argument; custom solver types would need to
be updated, but the public solver API is unchanged and the default
behaviour is bit-identical.

Verification: a 1D function with a smooth high-frequency perturbation
of order 1e-5 is solved from an initial point where the perturbation
makes the default Jacobian negative.  With the default step the solver
is deflected away from the root (`x = -0.749`), while with
`epsrel = 1e-3` it reaches it (`|x - 1| < 1e-3`).  The default value,
the rejection of a non-positive step with `GSL_EDOM` and the stored
value are also checked.  Negative control: making the setter ignore its
argument fails the tuned check, while the restored tree is clean.  Full
ctest 56/56 on MSVC x64.

Files: `multiroots/gsl_multiroots.h`, `multiroots/fsolver.c`,
`multiroots/broyden.c`, `multiroots/dnewton.c`, `multiroots/hybrid.c`,
`multiroots/test.c`, both manuals.

See Savannah bug #45782.


## [upstream] specfunc: complex zeta, eta and Hurwitz zeta functions

Commit: 2708ca0fa (documentation: a605f0c44)

The library had the real Riemann, Hurwitz and eta functions only.
Savannah bug #57173 (2019) asks for complex arguments.  The new
`specfunc/zeta_complex.c` adds

    gsl_sf_complex_zeta_e(x, y, &re, &im)
    gsl_sf_complex_hzeta_e(x, y, q, &re, &im)
    gsl_sf_complex_eta_e(x, y, &re, &im)

for `s = x + iy`, following the GSL complex special-function convention
of separate real and imaginary `gsl_sf_result` values.

For `Re(s) >= 0` the Hurwitz zeta function is evaluated with the
Euler-Maclaurin summation formula of `specfunc/zeta.c` generalised to
complex `s`; the number of leading terms is scaled with `|s|`, which is
what the critical strip needs.  For `Re(s) < 0` the Riemann zeta
function uses the reflection formula
`zeta(s) = 2^s pi^(s-1) sin(pi s/2) Gamma(1-s) zeta(1-s)`, with the
trivial zeros at the negative even integers returned exactly.  `eta`
uses `(1 - 2^(1-s)) zeta(s)` and handles `s = 1` exactly
(`eta(1) = log 2`).  The complex Hurwitz zeta is defined for `Re(s) > 1`,
matching the domain of the real `gsl_sf_hzeta_e`.

Verification: reference values are 30-digit `mpmath` values, and a
7000-point sweep over `Re(s) in [-30,30]`, `Im(s) in [-40,40]` gives a
worst relative error of 1.7e-13 for zeta, 1.8e-13 for eta and 5.2e-14
for hzeta, with the reported error bars covering the true error at every
point.  Negative control: flipping the sign of the Euler-Maclaurin
endpoint term fails the new checks, while the restored tree is clean.
Full ctest 56/56 on MSVC x64.

Files: `specfunc/zeta_complex.c`, `specfunc/gsl_sf_zeta.h`,
`specfunc/Makefile.am`, `cmake/gsl_sources.cmake`, `specfunc/test_sf.c`,
`specfunc/test_sf.h`, both manuals.

